    rdfs:subClassOf emmo:EMMO_1d0064d2_d77e_4ec7_b74a_00f98616156f ;
    skos:prefLabel "DielectricAndFerroicFunctionalMaterial"@en ;
    emmo:elucidation "Materials that rely on the alignment and manipulation of internal electric dipoles, magnetic moments, or spontaneous lattice strains."@en .

emmo:EMMO_d8a7aac9_984a_4254_ab3b_7d8b1c061149 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "MaterialByForceResponse"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf emmo:EMMO_4207e895_8b83_4318_996a_72cfb32acd94 ;
    skos:prefLabel "MaterialByForceResponse"@en ;
    emmo:elucidation "A classification system categorization of materials based on their constitutive behavior, energy storage, or deformation mechanisms when subjected to external physical fields or mechanical loads."@en .

emmo:EMMO_f1025834_0cd2_42a1_bfeb_13bec41c8655 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "CrystallineMaterial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://dictionary.iucr.org/Crystal> ;
    rdfs:subClassOf emmo:EMMO_220b7201_d277_4dca_bf6a_5a5e2c4062dd,
        emmo:EMMO_f00fb163_48c9_4c59_b6c2_4205c082aa54 ;
    skos:prefLabel "CrystallineMaterial"@en ;
    emmo:elucidation "A substance is in a crystal material if it has essentially a sharp diffraction pattern. The word essentially means that most of the intensity of the diffraction is concentrated in relatively sharp Bragg peaks, besides the always present diffuse scattering."@en ;
    emmo:note """A crystalline material is always condensed matter since it cannot be gaseous. In fact, in a gas we observe:
a) Thermal Dominance: The kinetic energy kB T of the particles completely overwhelms any attractive intermolecular forces
b) Mean Free Path: Gas particles are separated by vast distances relative to their size and are in constant, chaotic, random motion. They collide and scatter continuously.
Because a gas has absolutely zero correlation between the position and orientation of neighboring particles over time, it possesses no translational or orientational order which is the definition of a crystalline state."""@en,
        """Crystalline material can be solid or liquid. In fact, crystallinity is defined by atomic or molecular order, not by mechanical rigidity. Historically, "crystal" and "solid" were used interchangeably because the most common materials with long-range atomic order happen to be rigid.
However, the modern definition focuses entirely on symmetry and diffraction patterns. If a material has a regular, repeating spatial arrangement that produces a sharp, discrete X-ray diffraction pattern, it possesses crystallinity. If a material can permanently resist a shear force without flowing, it is mechanically a solid. Because these two properties are governed by different physical mechanisms, they can be decoupled."""@en,
        "Crystalline material does not imply that the entity is a single crystal, but only that its parts are in crystalline state (single crystal). Thus, a poly crystalline entity, made of crystal grains packed together is 99% a crystalline material, even if they are not oriented in the same way."@en .

dcmitype:Text a owl:Class .

emmo:EMMO_3cc35b7f_0d8a_40b4_89dd_07ec47f04093 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "CastIron"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf emmo:EMMO_621bbbf3_d357_4ea7_b235_bc6a9ad3366a ;
    skos:prefLabel "CastIron"@en .

emmo:EMMO_cc6988f0_136a_4933_b986_270cff5e1137 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "MagneticResponseMaterial"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf emmo:EMMO_d8a7aac9_984a_4254_ab3b_7d8b1c061149 ;
    skos:prefLabel "MagneticResponseMaterial"@en ;
    emmo:elucidation "Materials classified by the orientation, coupling, and net macroscopic alignment of their atomic magnetic moments under an external magnetic field force."@en .

emmo:EMMO_d4d9fa8b_5998_4398_8aa4_05bdafa83e11 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "MechanicalResponseMaterial"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf emmo:EMMO_d8a7aac9_984a_4254_ab3b_7d8b1c061149 ;
    skos:prefLabel "MechanicalResponseMaterial"@en ;
    emmo:elucidation "Materials classified by their structural deformation and energy dissipation behaviors when subjected to mechanical tensile, compressive, or shear stress."@en .

emmo:EMMO_1d0064d2_d77e_4ec7_b74a_00f98616156f a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "FunctionalMaterial"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf emmo:EMMO_8820f251_ad36_43f4_a693_c0e86a89cc1f ;
    skos:prefLabel "FunctionalMaterial"@en ;
    emmo:elucidation "A functional material is any material that exhibits distinct, reproducible changes in its electronic, magnetic, optical, chemical, or thermal properties when exposed to an external field or environmental change."@en ;
    emmo:note "Materials categorised for what they do rather than what they hold up. They are designed to react to their environment, convert energy, or process information."@en .

emmo:EMMO_2b43c6ff_4a2d_4eb1_9797_e279c52894d3 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "NonFerrousAlloy"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf emmo:EMMO_00ed0f82_f20e_4749_9780_380f43398466 ;
    skos:prefLabel "NonFerrousAlloy"@en ;
    emmo:elucidation "A metal alloy in which iron is not the primary constituent."@en .

emmo:EMMO_4207e895_8b83_4318_996a_72cfb32acd94 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Material"@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <urn:isbn:9780080966656>,
        <urn:isbn:9780471736967> ;
    rdfs:subClassOf emmo:EMMO_220b7201_d277_4dca_bf6a_5a5e2c4062dd,
        emmo:EMMO_6e9cb807_fc68_4bcf_b3ba_5fccc887c644 ;
    skos:prefLabel "Material"@en ;
    emmo:conceptualisation "It is quite hard to define what a material is, given the wide range of use of such term, especially in order to distinguish it from the more general matter concept. First, we decided to restrict the term's domain with respect to matter, by making a material always an ordinary and condensed matter entity, thus excluding antimatter, particulate matter (e.g., molecular entities such as atoms and molecules), and mesoscopic entities (e.g., nanoparticles). Then, to call material every entity that can be categorised according to the typical criteria used in material science (e.g., by structure, function, size, isotropy). Such criteria usually categorise a substance according to kind, quality or quantity and are expressed in order to facilitate the identification of specific class of substances according to an intended use. In short, a material is what is called material in material science."@en ;
    emmo:elucidation "A material is condensed matter categorized according to some criteria based on kind, quality or quantity. An intended use is typically associated with each of the classes used to categorize a material entity."@en ;
    emmo:note "A instance of a material (e.g. nitrogen) can represent any state of matter. The fact that the individual also belongs to other classes (e.g. liquid) would reveal the actual form in which the material is found."@en,
        "A material may be either a fluid or a solid substance. Gas is not a material, because the material concept is here restricted to classical material science, focusing exclusively on condensed matter."@en,
        """Materials are here restricted to condensed matter. The core paradigm of materials science is the Structure-Property-Processing-Performance relationship. This paradigm relies entirely on atoms being close enough to interact continuously, thus excluding non-condensed matter from the materials field:
- Structure: refers to crystal lattices, defects, grain boundaries, or amorphous molecular networks. Gases, by definition, lack any long-range or short-range structural arrangement because their particles are in constant, random motion.
- Properties: materials science measures collective mechanical properties (elastic modulus, hardness), thermal properties (conductivity), and electrical properties (band gaps). A gas cannot support a shear stress, deform plastically, or possess a structural microstructure.
Because gases lack a fixed structure, they are traditionally handed over to chemistry, thermodynamics, or fluid mechanics, rather than materials science."""@en,
        "The concept of material is not an ontological one i.e., does not originate from the entity per se, but originates from actual materials science practitioners and their categorisation habits. For this reason, all materials subclasses are grouped according to classification criteria (e.g., by compactness, by function, by size)."@en .

emmo:EMMO_c5e9e390_8836_4440_b4fd_c906fdd4f438 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "CeramicMaterial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <urn:isbn:9780471736967> ;
    rdfs:subClassOf emmo:EMMO_4db521a3_f80f_436b_b9c7_e4b52b1383ea ;
    skos:altLabel "Ceramic"@en ;
    skos:example "Al₂O₃ based compunds"@en,
        "Concrete"@en,
        "Glass"@en,
        "SiC or Si₃N₄ based compunds"@en,
        "ZrO₂ based compunds"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "CeramicMaterial"@en ;
    emmo:conceptualisation "Ceramic is defined commonly as an inorganic non-metallic substance made from elements (metals, non-metals, semi-metals like silicon) combined into compounds. Ceramics are mostly oxides, nitrides and carbides."@en ;
    emmo:elucidation "An inorganic non-metallic substance made from elements (metals, non-metals, semi-metals like silicon) combined into compounds (oxides, carbides, nitrides) that are typically hard, strong, brittle, corrosion-resistant, and good insulators."@en ;
    emmo:note """A ceramic with a disordered, random structure frozen in place, is considered amorphous. An amorphous ceramic can be categorised according to its formation process, depending on the fact that it went or not through a glass transition in glass or amorphous solid.
The distinction is about the thermodynamic state of the material. Because a true glass was once a liquid, it occupies a specific metastable energy state dictated by how fast it was cooled. If you take an amorphous solid thin film (e.g., made by PVD) and heat it up, it will often crystallize abruptly or behave unpredictably because it has a highly stressed, ultra-high-energy structure. A true glass, when heated, will predictably soften into a supercooled liquid once it hits its Tg."""@en,
        """While the word "ceramic" instantly brings to mind brittle, rigid objects like coffee mugs, brick, or technical alumina plates, a ceramic is defined purely by its chemical bonds and atomic structure, as an inorganic non-metallic substance.
Here we embrace this definition in its wide and literal meaning, even if in practical cases the term ceramic is restricted only to high-temperature, corrosion and wear resistant materials."""@en .

